 "productionNotes": "No two sources consulted here agree on how many first-generation Challengers were built, and the disagreement is largest in the year that matters most. For 1970, Wikipedia's production table gives 76,935, the figure that reconciles with its widely repeated 165,437 first-generation total. Muscle Car Facts gives 83,032, split as 59,921 base hardtops including SE cars, 3,173 base convertibles, 18,868 R/T hardtops including SE cars and 1,070 R/T convertibles. Over-Drive Magazine's breakdown - 53,337 base hardtops, 6,584 base SE, 3,173 base convertibles, 14,899 R/T coupes, 3,979 R/T SE, 1,070 R/T convertibles and 2,399 T/As - sums to 85,441. RM Sotheby's catalogue copy says only 'more than 84,000'. The R/T figure is firmer at 19,938, stated by RM Sotheby's and matched by Muscle Car Facts' arithmetic.\n\nFor 1971 the split is between roughly 26,300 and roughly 29,900. Wikipedia gives 26,299; Over-Drive Magazine and Curbside Classic give 29,883; conceptcarz prints 29,803 as its own total while quoting 26,299 in the same article's year-on-year comparison. All agree on 4,630 R/Ts. For 1972 Wikipedia gives 22,919 against Curbside Classic's 26,658, the latter including 8,123 Rallyes. Only 1973 and 1974 are quiet: 27,930 and 11,354, with production stopping in late April 1974.\n\nEngine-level counts come from registry work rather than factory records. RM Sotheby's, citing Galen Govier, states 287 US-specification 426 Hemi hardtops for 1970 of which 137 were four-speeds, and 70 Hemi Challengers for 1971 of which 58 were four-speeds, with 49 of the 1971 cars then known to survive. The Challenger Registry is cited for approximately 1,640 V-code 440 Six Pack cars in 1970. Because the model-year totals conflict, no single production figure is asserted.",
